the first thing you notice when you slip  behind the wheel of the BMW i3 is how  superior you feel physically and morally  first of all even though it's a small  car the seat is deceptively high  certainly above the one in its big  brother the iead supercar the i3 doesn't  feel at all muscley engine wise but the  power trip you get from sitting higher  than expected is worth noting and as for  its moral superiority that comes from  the cars electric battery which gets  seventy miles per charge  you're not just driving in high style  you're saving the world and it will cost  you less than $50,000 here's what you  need to know the car was launched in  2013 as part of BMWs electric range of  cars which they call the I program the  version I drove has an all-electric 170  horsepower motor paired with a range  extending two cylinder engine  combine them and you'll get about 117  miles per gallon you can plug it into  any outlet like you would plug in a  vacuum but using the fast charging  station that BMW also sells really  speeds things up on average it takes  four hours to recharge fully the i3 is  silent as it runs and it's very smooth  to drive but don't plan on winning any  drag races in this thing it takes seven  seconds to hit 60 miles per hour and it  has a max speed of barely over 90 miles  per hour slowing down in the i3 is  pretty rough too the minute you take  your foot off the gas the car basically  stops you feel like you're braking but  you're not in fact braking seems almost  redundant at this point driving in  comfort mode makes it feel smoother but  that reduces the battery charge by half  the interior of the carbon-fiber  passenger shell feels spacious if a  little flat I like the gray wool cloth  seats paired with faux bamboo now we've  got to talk about something that is not  as shallow as it seems how this car  looks to which I answer not great  remember the i8 with its futuristic  angled doors swooping fenders and  muscular haunches it looks so cool the  i3 is the opposite of that it's tall and  boxy and its rear doors open bizarrely  backwards is there a rule that says  smaller less expensive electric cars  have to look weird I don't get it I  guess if you're saving the world you  just don't care what you look like
 

